<!--

    Copyright (C) 2026 Antony Stubbs and contributors

    SpotBugs exclusions for the direct transport (astubbs#242; the policy is in
    docs/client-static-analysis.md). The gate itself is bound in the parent pom.

    One entry, naming one class, one method and one pattern. Every entry here is a judgement, not a
    mute: if you are adding one, write why, or fix the code. A module-wide or pattern-wide exclusion
    is the thing to refuse - it silences the finding for code nobody has written yet.

-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <!--
        THE FINDING'S OWN REMEDY IS FORBIDDEN HERE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS DECLINED RATHER THAN FIXED.
        fb-contrib's CE_CLASS_ENVY says toInboundRecord calls more methods on RecordContext than on
        its own class, and the refactor it is pointing at is "move this method onto RecordContext".
        That is precisely the dependency this architecture forbids: RecordContext is a
        parallel-consumer-core type, and core must not know that a client API exists. The mirror rule
        is enforced from the other side by ClientSurfaceArchTest, which fails the api module's build
        if a core or Kafka type reaches the shared surface.

        A translation method between two representations reads the source object and nothing else -
        that is what makes it a translation rather than logic. The gRPC transport's WireMapping is
        the same shape for the same reason; it does not trip the detector only because its
        conversions are shorter, which is not a difference worth restructuring code around.

        Scoped to the one method, so any OTHER method on this class that grows real feature envy is
        still reported.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="bz.stub.parallelconsumer.client.direct.DirectParallelConsumerClient"/>
        <Method name="toInboundRecord"/>
        <Bug pattern="CE_CLASS_ENVY"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
